-- ccc2-riots.l4 -- Criminal Code (Canada), PART II, ss 63 to 69 ("Unlawful Assemblies and Riots"): unlawful assembly, riot, their punishment, the reading of the
-- proclamation, offences related to the proclamation, neglect by a peace officer. Layer 1 (the law, objectively). Source: a plain-text rendering, made by a
-- converter script, of the Justice Laws Website consolidation (current to 2026-09-30); it is not the official text, and nothing here is claimed verified against the official text.
-- Reading a result: JUST TRUE / JUST FALSE (the text says no in its own words) / NOTHING (not covered): see the header of ccc2-treason.l4 and NOTES.md.
-- The periods in section 68 are in minutes, as the text counts them.

§ `Section 63 -- Unlawful assembly`

-- Subsection (1): "An unlawful assembly is an assembly of three or more persons who, with intent to carry out any common purpose, assemble in such a manner or so conduct themselves when they are
-- assembled as to cause persons in the neighbourhood of the assembly to fear, on reasonable grounds, that they (a) will disturb the peace tumultuously; or (b) will by that assembly needlessly and
-- without reasonable cause provoke other persons to disturb the peace tumultuously." A definition ("is"): a case outside it is JUST FALSE.
-- src:1060 | Unlawful assembly
-- src:1061 | 63 (1) An unlawful assembly is an assembly of three or more persons who, with intent to carry out any common purpose, assemble in such a manner or so conduct themselves when they are assembled as to cause persons in the neighbourhood of the assembly to fear, on reasonable grounds, that they
-- src:1062 | (a) will disturb the peace tumultuously; or
-- src:1063 | (b) will by that assembly needlessly and without reasonable cause provoke other persons to disturb the peace tumultuously.
@ref Criminal Code s 63(1) (unlawful assembly) (src:1060-1063)
GIVEN `the number of persons assembled` IS A NUMBER
      `the persons assemble with intent to carry out a common purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the persons assemble in such a manner, or so conduct themselves when assembled, as to cause persons in the neighbourhood of the assembly to fear` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the fear is on reasonable grounds` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the fear is that they will disturb the peace tumultuously` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the fear is that they will by that assembly needlessly and without reasonable cause provoke other persons to disturb the peace tumultuously` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(1) the assembly is an unlawful assembly` MEANS
    IF `the number of persons assembled` AT LEAST 3
        AND `the persons assemble with intent to carry out a common purpose`
        AND `the persons assemble in such a manner, or so conduct themselves when assembled, as to cause persons in the neighbourhood of the assembly to fear`
        AND `the fear is on reasonable grounds`
        AND (`the fear is that they will disturb the peace tumultuously` OR `the fear is that they will by that assembly needlessly and without reasonable cause provoke other persons to disturb the peace tumultuously`)
    THEN JUST TRUE
    ELSE JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "An unlawful assembly is an assembly of three or more persons who"

-- Subsection (2): "Persons who are lawfully assembled may become an unlawful assembly if they conduct themselves with a common purpose in a manner that would have made the assembly unlawful if they had
-- assembled in that manner for that purpose." A function named for a power ("may become") whose "if" gate fails answers NOTHING, not JUST FALSE (ruling of 2026-11-12, limit (1); fork F9).
-- src:1064 | Lawful assembly becoming unlawful
-- src:1065 | (2) Persons who are lawfully assembled may become an unlawful assembly if they conduct themselves with a common purpose in a manner that would have made the assembly unlawful if they had assembled in that manner for that purpose.
@ref Criminal Code s 63(2) (lawful assembly becoming unlawful) (src:1064-1065)
GIVEN `the persons are lawfully assembled` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the persons conduct themselves with a common purpose` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 63(1) the assembly would have been an unlawful assembly had they assembled in that manner for that purpose` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(2) persons who are lawfully assembled may become an unlawful assembly` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF NOT `the persons are lawfully assembled` THEN NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "Persons who are lawfully assembled"
        IF `the persons conduct themselves with a common purpose` AND `s 63(1) the assembly would have been an unlawful assembly had they assembled in that manner for that purpose` EQUALS JUST TRUE THEN JUST TRUE
        OTHERWISE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (limited by its own wording): "if they conduct themselves with a common purpose in a manner that would have made the assembly unlawful" (a grant named "may become": the failed "if" is not turned into a bar)

-- Subsection (3): "Persons are not unlawfully assembled by reason only that they are assembled to protect the dwelling-house of any one of them against persons who are threatening to break and enter
-- it for the purpose of committing an indictable offence therein." A flat negative (JUST FALSE); "dwelling-house" is s 2's term (a seam input).
-- src:1066 | Exception
-- src:1067 | (3) Persons are not unlawfully assembled by reason only that they are assembled to protect the dwelling-house of any one of them against persons who are threatening to break and enter it for the purpose of committing an indictable offence therein.
@ref Criminal Code s 63(3) (exception) (src:1066-1067)
GIVEN `the persons are assembled to protect the dwelling-house of any one of them` IS A BOOLEAN
      `s 2 dwelling-house: the place is a dwelling-house` IS A MAYBE BOOLEAN
      `the persons they protect it against are threatening to break and enter it for the purpose of committing an indictable offence therein` IS A BOOLEAN
      `that is the only reason they could be unlawfully assembled` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A MAYBE BOOLEAN
`s 63(3) the persons are unlawfully assembled by reason only that they are assembled to protect a dwelling-house` MEANS
    IF `the persons are assembled to protect the dwelling-house of any one of them`
        AND `s 2 dwelling-house: the place is a dwelling-house` EQUALS JUST TRUE
        AND `the persons they protect it against are threatening to break and enter it for the purpose of committing an indictable offence therein`
        AND `that is the only reason they could be unlawfully assembled`
    THEN JUST FALSE   -- FALSE-kind (the text says no in its own words): "Persons are not unlawfully assembled by reason only that they are assembled to protect the dwelling-house of any one of them against persons who are threatening to break and enter it for the purpose of committing an indictable offence therein"
    ELSE NOTHING   -- NOTHING-kind (silent): the subsection says no only for this ground

